117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (Income level prediction is an important socio-economic classification problem with applications in taxation analysis, labor market research, credit risk assessment, and public policy planning. The Adult Data Set provides demographic and employment-related attributes to predict whether an individual earns more than $50K per year.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (Prior studies have demonstrated competitive classification performance on this dataset using traditional machine learning models. However, most works primarily emphasize predictive accuracy without comprehensive hyperparameter optimization, multi-metric evaluation, or fairness assessment.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (Since the dataset contains sensitive attributes such as sex and race, predictive modeling also raises concerns regarding algorithmic bias. Therefore, there is a need for an integrated machine learning framework that simultaneously optimizes predictive performance and evaluates fairness across demographic groups.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 699 0 R
/T (Literature Review)
/E (Literature Review)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (The Adult Data Set has been widely used for benchmarking supervised learning algorithms. Ron Kohavi \(1996\) compared Naive Bayes and decision tree classifiers, emphasizing the importance of cross-validation for reliable performance estimation. Pedro Domingos \(1999\) introduced cost-sensitive classification techniques, demonstrating improved decision performance under asymmetric misclassification costs.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (Chakrabarty \(2018\) conducted a comparative study of Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting classifiers on the Adult dataset, reporting approximately 88% accuracy using Gradient Boosting. The study provided a strong benchmark but relied primarily on accuracy as the evaluation metric and did not extensively explore systematic hyperparameter tuning.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (Collectively, these studies highlight strong baselines in predictive modeling and fairness-aware learning, but limited integration of systematic hyperparameter optimization, multi-metric evaluation, and fairness assessment within a unified machine learning pipeline.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
